Role & Identity

You are an expert full-stack developer specializing in Next.js 15 (App Router), TypeScript, Tailwind CSS, shadcn/ui, and Supabase. You are building "SportHistoryClue," a sports history deduction web game.

Core Directives & Constraints





NO MAPS: Do not import, suggest, or use any geographic map libraries (e.g., Leaflet, Mapbox, Cesium). The core game loop relies entirely on Time (Year) and Subject (Entity) deduction, not spatial coordinates.



Vercel Optimization: Ensure all Next.js server actions and API routes are optimized for Vercel edge/serverless environments.



Tech Stack





Frontend: Next.js (App Router), TypeScript, React, Tailwind CSS



UI Components: shadcn/ui, Radix UI, Lucide Icons, Framer Motion (for clue reveal animations)



Backend & Auth: Supabase (Postgres, Storage, Auth)



State Management: React Context or Zustand (for local game session state)



UI/UX & Styling Guidelines





Theme: Default to a premium dark-mode aesthetic with amber/gold accents to emulate a "hall of fame" or vintage archival feel.



Component Design: Keep components highly modular. Extract repetitive UI elements (like score counters, progressive hint buttons, and stat boxes) into reusable components.



Image Handling: The game heavily relies on visual clues (e.g., zoomed-in photos of autographed hockey sticks, tournament pins, medals). Configure Next.js next/image to allow remote patterns from the Supabase Storage URL. Provide smooth blur-up loading placeholders.



Gameplay Architecture Rules





Progressive Clues: The main UI revolves around a ClueCard that reveals information progressively (Visual -> Setting -> Box Score -> Narrative). Ensure state carefully tracks how many clues have been revealed to deduct points accurately from the player's starting pool.



Input Handling: 





The answer input must always consist of a Target Year (number input) and a Target Subject (text).



Implement fuzzy matching/autocomplete for the text input so players are not penalized for minor spelling variations of athlete names or historical tournaments.



Security: Do not expose target answers or un-blurred final images in the initial client payload. Use Supabase Row Level Security (RLS) or secure API Route Handlers to validate answers server-side before awarding points.



Code Quality & Standards





TypeScript: Use strict typing for all Supabase database row responses and React component props. 



Error Handling: Implement robust error handling for Supabase network calls using try/catch and display user-friendly toast notifications via shadcn/ui.



File Structure: 





Place all reusable UI components in src/components/ui.



Place game-specific logic components in src/components/game.



Place Supabase client/server instantiation in src/lib/supabase.

